      },
      code: "def relu(v):\n    return [max(0.0, x) for x in v]\n\nW1 = [[1.0, -1.0], [0.5, 2.0]]   # 2 hidden units x 2 inputs\nb1 = [0.0, 1.0]\nW2 = [1.0, 3.0]                  # 1 output x 2 hidden\nb2 = -1.0\n\ndef mlp_forward(x, use_relu=True):\n    z = [sum(x[i] * W1[i][j] for i in range(2)) + b1[j] for j in range(2)]\n    h = relu(z) if use_relu else z\n    return sum(h[i] * W2[i] for i in range(2)) + b2\n\nfor x in ([2.0, 1.0], [0.0, 0.0], [0.0, -2.0]):\n    z = [sum(x[i] * W1[i][j] for i in range(2)) + b1[j] for j in range(2)]\n    print('x=%s z=%s h=%s | o(relu)=%.1f o(no-act)=%.1f'\n          % (x, z, relu(z), mlp_forward(x, True), mlp_forward(x, False)))\n\nWc = [sum(W1[i][k] * W2[k] for k in range(2)) for i in range(2)]\nbc = sum(b1[k] * W2[k] for k in range(2)) + b2\nprint('collapsed into one layer: W=%s b=%.1f' % (Wc, bc))\n\nimport math\nxs = [-2.0, -1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 2.0]\nsig = [1.0 / (1.0 + math.exp(-v)) for v in xs]\ntah = [math.tanh(v) for v in xs]\nprint('x      :', xs)\nprint('relu   :', relu(xs))\nprint('sigmoid:', ['%.4f' % v for v in sig])\nprint('tanh   :', ['%.4f' % v for v in tah])\ns4 = (1.0 / (1.0 + math.exp(-4.0))) * (1.0 - 1.0 / (1.0 + math.exp(-4.0)))\nprint('sigmoid grad @0 = %.2f | @4 = %.4f' % (0.25, s4))\nprint('params: W1(4) + b1(2) + W2(2) + b2(1) = %d' % (4 + 2 + 2 + 1))",
      explanation: {
        ar: 'المدخل [2, 1] ينتج z=[2.5, 1.0] وh=[2.5, 1.0] والناتج 4.5 — وبدون تنشيط الناتج نفسه هنا لأن z موجبة فReLU شفافة. لكن عند [0, −2] يظهر الفرق القاتل: z=[−1.0, −3.0]، فReLU يطفئها إلى h=[0, 0] والناتج −1.0، بينما الشبكة الأفينية تعطي −11.0. والطي ممكن رياضياً: W=W⁽¹⁾W⁽²⁾=[−2.0, 6.5] وb=2.0 يعيدان الناتج الأفيني نفسه من طبقة واحدة. جدول التنشيطات يظهر sigmoid يتشبع ومشتقه 0.25 كحد أقصى عند الصفر و0.0177 عند 4 — بذرة مشكلة تلاشي التدرجات في الدرس القادم. الشبكة كلها 9 معاملات.',
        en: 'Input [2, 1] produces z=[2.5, 1.0], h=[2.5, 1.0] and output 4.5 — identical without activation here because z is positive and ReLU is transparent. But at [0, −2] the fatal difference appears: z=[−1.0, −3.0], ReLU shuts them off to h=[0, 0] and the output is −1.0, while the affine network returns −11.0. Folding is possible mathematically: W=W⁽¹⁾W⁽²⁾=[−2.0, 6.5] and b=2.0 reproduce the same affine output from a single layer. The activation table shows sigmoid saturating with a derivative capped at 0.25 at zero and 0.0177 at 4 — the seed of next lesson vanishing-gradient story. The whole network has 9 parameters.',
